 (names values)←type resolveBits(names values);lc;bits;cmap;hi
⍝ Resolve a workspace-held raster into the CBits the client can draw.
⍝
⍝ A ⎕WC Bitmap or Icon may carry its image in the workspace as Bits (a matrix
⍝ of palette indices) plus CMap (an n-by-3 RGB palette), instead of naming a
⍝ File. Applications that pre-date image files do it this way -- the raster
⍝ travelled inside the .dws.
⍝
⍝ The client already renders CBits (a matrix of packed 24-bit values) onto a
⍝ canvas, so resolve the palette here, where both arrays are still APL.
⍝
⍝ Bits and CMap are deliberately left in place as well: ⎕WG'Bits' must keep
⍝ returning the matrix, because applications measure images with it rather than
⍝ carrying the dimensions separately.
 :If ~(⊂type)∊'bitmap' 'icon' ⋄ →0 ⋄ :EndIf
 lc←⎕C names
 :If (⊂'cbits')∊lc ⋄ →0 ⋄ :EndIf                  ⍝ caller supplied CBits itself
 :If ~((⊂'bits')∊lc)∧(⊂'cmap')∊lc ⋄ →0 ⋄ :EndIf
 bits←(lc⍳⊂'bits')⊃values
 cmap←(lc⍳⊂'cmap')⊃values
 :If (2≠≢⍴bits)∨(2≠≢⍴cmap)∨3≠¯1↑⍴cmap ⋄ →0 ⋄ :EndIf
 :If 0∊⍴bits ⋄ →0 ⋄ :EndIf
 :If (≢cmap)≤hi←⌈/,bits                           ⍝ index past the end of the palette
     Warning'Bitmap Bits index ',(⍕hi),' exceeds the ',(⍕≢cmap),'-entry CMap'
     →0
 :EndIf
⍝ Palette indices address CMap rows from 0 regardless of ⎕IO, hence the ⎕IO+.
⍝ Summing over the last axis avoids monadic ⍉, which would reverse all three
⍝ axes of the rank-3 intermediate and silently transpose the image.
 names,←⊂'CBits'
 values,←⊂+/cmap[⎕IO+bits;]×⍤1⊢65536 256 1
